From: GPT-6.1-Sol <noreply@openai.com>
Subject: [PATCH] misc: fastrpc: retain the DMA device for buffer lifetime

Finish audit F2's residual in 0188: a channel reference protects address
translation metadata, but not the raw device used by dma_free_coherent,
dma_get_sgtable and mmap after the allocating user or platform device is
gone. Each coherent buffer now owns a device reference through DMA free;
allocation failure drops it. No user reference/cycle is introduced.
This protects the device object; a live DMA/IOMMU domain after arbitrary
session-device unbind is still a separate hardware/core-lifecycle constraint.

Local follow-up, no matching upstream backport found. Hardware/disposable
KASAN kernel: allocate/export, close FastRPC FD, mmap/attach/close dma-buf;
repeat with rpmsg/service teardown and allocation failure. Session-device
unbind with a live exported buffer needs separate qualification.

diff --git a/drivers/misc/fastrpc.c b/drivers/misc/fastrpc.c
--- a/drivers/misc/fastrpc.c
+++ b/drivers/misc/fastrpc.c
@@ -429,6 +429,7 @@
 {
 	dma_free_coherent(buf->dev, buf->size, buf->virt,
 			  fastrpc_ipa_to_dma_addr(buf->cctx, buf->dma_addr));
+	put_device(buf->dev);
 	kfree(buf);
 }
 
@@ -449,13 +450,14 @@
 	buf->virt = NULL;
 	buf->dma_addr = 0;
 	buf->size = size;
-	buf->dev = dev;
+	buf->dev = get_device(dev);
 	buf->raddr = 0;
 
 	buf->virt = dma_alloc_coherent(dev, buf->size, &buf->dma_addr,
 				       GFP_KERNEL);
 	if (!buf->virt) {
 		mutex_destroy(&buf->lock);
+		put_device(buf->dev);
 		kfree(buf);
 		return -ENOMEM;
 	}
